      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Blend canned tomatoes, chillies and one onion until smooth."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat oil in a large pot over medium-high heat. Brown chicken pieces on both sides, about 3 minutes per side. Remove and set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "In the same pot, fry the sliced onion for 5 minutes. Add garlic and ginger, cook 1 minute."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add tomato puree and stir for 2 minutes. Pour in the blended tomato mixture and bay leaves. Cook for 15 minutes until the oil separates."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Stir in peanut butter and 500ml water. Mix well. Return chicken to the pot."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Simmer on medium-low for 25 minutes, stirring every 5 minutes. Add spinach in the last 5 minutes if using. Season with salt."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Stew is grainy",
              "cause": "Peanut butter not fully dissolved",
              "fix": "Whisk the peanut butter with a ladleful of the hot broth before adding it to the pot."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Stew is too thick",
              "cause": "Not enough water or cooked on too high heat",
              "fix": "Add water 50ml at a time and stir until the desired consistency is reached."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Groundnut soup (or stew) is made from Senegal to Kenya. In Mali it is called tigadegena and is often eaten without meat. In Ghana it is typically cooked with chicken and served over fufu. In Sudan a similar dish is called ful sudani. The dish moved with West African communities to the Caribbean and the American South, where peanut stew remains part of Gullah Geechee cooking."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
